middleware, session management, or frontend auth guards. ## Auth Flow

{{#if (eq stack.auth.strategy 'google-oauth')}}
  1. User clicks "Sign in with Google" 2. Frontend redirects to `/api/auth/google` 3. Server
  redirects to Google OAuth consent screen 4. Google redirects back to `/api/auth/google/callback`
  5. Server validates token, creates/finds user, issues
  {{stack.auth.session}}
  token 6. Frontend stores token and includes in subsequent requests
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq stack.auth.strategy 'email-password')}}
  1. User submits email + password to `/api/auth/register` or `/api/auth/login` 2. Server validates
  credentials, issues
  {{stack.auth.session}}
  token 3. Frontend stores token and includes in subsequent requests
{{/if}}

## Session:
{{stack.auth.session}}

{{#if (eq stack.auth.session 'jwt')}}
  - Store JWT in httpOnly secure cookie (preferred) or Authorization header - Token payload: `{
  userId, email, role }` - Token expiry: 15 min access token + 7 day refresh token - Refresh
  endpoint: `POST /api/auth/refresh`
{{/if}}

## Key Rules - Never store passwords in plain text — always hash with bcrypt (12 rounds) - Never
expose tokens in URLs or logs - Always validate token on every protected route - Use environment
variables for secrets (JWT_SECRET, GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID) ## References -
[auth-flow](references/auth-flow.md) - [middleware-template](references/middleware-template.md)
